WEBVTT

NOTE English visual guide. Editorial descriptions of on-screen concepts; no spoken narration.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
A recurrent neural network processes a sequence one item at a time, carrying a hidden state forward.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.600
Word order matters: “dog bites man” and “man bites dog” contain the same words but express different

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.200
events.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.600
Map each token to an embedding vector before passing it to the recurrent computation.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.600
At each step, combine the current input with the previous hidden state using learned weights and an

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.200
activation.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.400
The updated hidden state carries information into the next step. The recurrent parameters are shared

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.800
across time.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.800
Unrolling draws the same computation repeatedly, making the sequence of dependencies visible.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.800
A prediction head converts a hidden state into scores for the next token; softmax turns those scores

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.600
into probabilities.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.800
Compare the next-token prediction with the true next token using cross-entropy loss.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.400
A later prediction may need information that appeared several steps earlier.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.400
The fading memory bars illustrate a difficulty; they are not measurements of the contribution of

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.800
each word.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Backpropagation through time follows the dependencies backward to compute gradients for the shared

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.000
weights.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.400
Repeated multiplication can shrink gradients. Multiplying by one half repeatedly is an illustrative

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.800
example, not a law that every recurrent gradient follows.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.400
Updates based on weak long-range gradients may struggle to teach the network which earlier

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.800
information to retain.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.200
LSTMs and GRUs introduce gates that regulate what to retain, overwrite or expose.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.800
The next section examines these gated architectures. They improve the ability to learn longer

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.600
dependencies, while retaining sequential computation.
